Upon receiving an AUTHENTICATION FAILURE message with 5GMM cause #21 "synch failure", the network:

1. **Stops timer T3560** (Clause 5.4.1.3.4);  
2. **Uses the AUTS parameter** included in the Authentication failure parameter IE (Clause 5.4.1.3.7 f, Clause 8.2.4.2) to re-synchronise the sequence number (SQN);  
3. **Deletes all unused authentication vectors** for the SUPI and **requests new authentication vectors** from the UDM/AUSF;  
4. Upon successful re-synchronisation, **re-initiates the 5G AKA based primary authentication and key agreement procedure**, sending a new AUTHENTICATION REQUEST containing a valid SQN and MAC (Clause 5.4.1.3.7 f);  
5. Optionally, upon receipt of **two consecutive** AUTHENTICATION FAILURE messages with cause #21, the network **may terminate** the procedure by sending AUTHENTICATION REJECT (Clause 5.4.1.3.7 f, NOTE4).

When the registration attempt counter is reset, the UE:

- Stops timer **T3519**, if running;  
- Deletes any stored **SUCI**;  
- (If applicable) Resets the **registration attempt counter for non-3GPP access**, depending on context (e.g., not impacted by EPS attach per Note 2 in Clause 5.5.1.1);  
- May trigger re-evaluation of registration eligibility (e.g., enabling retry after back-off or PLMN change), but no further automatic NAS procedure initiation is mandated solely by the reset — subsequent behavior depends on 5GMM state and update status.

This is derived from Clause 5.5.1.1, which explicitly lists the reset conditions and specifies these two mandatory actions (stop T3519, delete SUCI) upon reset.

Upon detecting a MAC failure (5GMM cause #20) after receiving an AUTHENTICATION REQUEST, the UE shall:

1. Send an **AUTHENTICATION FAILURE** message with 5GMM cause #20 ("MAC failure") to the network;  
2. Start timer **T3520**;  
3. Stop any running retransmission timers (e.g., T3510, T3517, T3521);  
4. Delete any previously stored RAND and RES*;  
5. Stop timer T3516, if running.

The UE then follows the abnormal case procedure in Clause 5.4.1.3.7 item c — including potential handling of an IDENTITY REQUEST (to provide SUCI), processing a subsequent AUTHENTICATION REQUEST (if sent upon GUTI–SUPI mapping correction), or treating the network as failed if T3520 expires or three consecutive failures occur (per emergency/non-emergency rules).

The periodic registration update procedure for 5GS services is controlled within the UE by the **periodic registration update timer T3512**, which is:

- **Started/reset** upon receipt of a `REGISTRATION ACCEPT` message containing a non-zero, non-deactivated T3512 value (Clause 5.3.7);
- **Only applicable over 3GPP access** — *not performed for non-3GPP access* (Clause 5.3.7, Clause 5.5.1.3.1);
- **Reset and restarted** with its initial value when the UE transitions from 5GMM-CONNECTED to 5GMM-IDLE mode *unless* “strictly periodic registration timer supported” is indicated in the MICO IE (in which case it runs continuously across mode changes) (Clause 5.3.7);
- **Stopped** when the UE enters 5GMM-CONNECTED mode or 5GMM-DEREGISTERED state over 3GPP access (Clause 5.3.7);
- **Triggered upon expiry**: the UE in 5GMM-IDLE mode initiates the *registration procedure for mobility and periodic registration update*; in 5GMM-CONNECTED mode, it resets and restarts T3512 (Clause 5.3.7);
- **Not initiated** if the UE is registered *only for emergency services* — instead, it locally deregisters (Clause 5.3.7).

Thus, T3512 is the sole UE-side control mechanism for periodic registration updates, strictly governed by access type (3GPP-only), registration state, and network-provided configuration.

The UE-DS-TT residence time IE must be included in the message if:

1. The UE supports transfer of port management information containers;  
2. The UE-DS-TT residence time is available at the UE.

The AMF must include:  
1. **EAP message IE** — sourced from the EAP-success or EAP-failure message provided by the AAA-S via the NSSAAF (Clause 5.4.7.3.1);  
2. **S-NSSAI IE** — sourced from the HPLMN S-NSSAI or SNPN S-NSSAI associated with the authentication result (Clause 5.4.7.3.1).

The network includes the T3448 value IE if:

1. The UE supports control plane CIoT 5GS optimizations;  
2. The network activates congestion control for transport of user data via the control plane.

The purpose of the 5G AKA based primary authentication and key agreement procedure is to provide **mutual authentication** between the UE and the network and to **derive and agree upon the keys** **KAUSF**, **KSEAF**, and **KAMF**, as defined in TS 33.501. These keys are essential for establishing the native 5G NAS security context:  
- **KAUSF** (Key Authentication Server Function) is used by the AUSF for subsequent authentication;  
- **KSEAF** (Key Serving Network Entity Authentication Function) is used by the AMF for deriving KAMF and other keys;  
- **KAMF** (Key Access Management Function) serves as the root key for the 5GS NAS integrity protection and ciphering key hierarchy and is stored in the partial native 5G NAS security context in both UE and network.

The emergency numbers in the **Emergency number list IE** are valid **only in PLMNs of the same country** as the PLMN that provided the list (Cl. 5.3.12, 5.3.12A; Cl. 8.2.7.19).

The emergency numbers in the **Extended emergency number list IE** are valid:
- **In the same country** as the providing PLMN — *if* the EENLV field indicates “valid in the country”;
- **Only in the specific PLMN (or SNPN)** from which the IE is received — *if* the EENLV field indicates “valid only in the PLMN/SNPN”;
- **Only in the SNPN** from which the IE is received — *regardless of the EENLV value*, when received from an SNPN (Cl. 5.3.12, 5.3.12A; Cl. 8.2.7.20).

The UE must:
- Send an **AUTHENTICATION FAILURE** message to the network with **5GMM cause #71 "ngKSI already in use"**;
- Start **timer T3520**;
- Stop any running retransmission timers (e.g., T3510, T3517, T3521);
- Delete any previously stored **RAND** and **RES\***;
- Stop **timer T3516**, if running.

(Per Clauses 5.4.1.3.6 and 5.4.1.3.7(e), and confirmed by 5.4.1.2.4.5(a).)

Upon receiving a **PDU SESSION MODIFICATION COMMAND** message containing a PDU session ID that corresponds to a PDU session in state **PDU SESSION INACTIVE**, the UE shall:

- Set the **5GSM cause IE to #43 "Invalid PDU session identity"** in a **5GSM STATUS** message;  
- Include the **received PDU session ID** in the **PDU session ID IE** of that 5GSM STATUS message;  
- Transport the 5GSM STATUS message via **UL NAS TRANSPORT** (as per subclause 5.4.5);  
- Take **no further action** on the modification command (i.e., does not process timers, QoS rules, or other IEs in the command).

This behavior is explicitly defined in **Clause 6.3.2.6(a)** — the sole and mandatory response for this abnormal case. No PDU SESSION MODIFICATION COMPLETE/REJECT is sent; only the 5GSM STATUS message with cause #43 is generated.

The AMF sends the **Configured NSSAI IE** to the UE for the current SNPN **to provision the UE with a network-configured set of S-NSSAIs applicable to that SNPN**, *provided the UE is neither registering nor registered for onboarding services in SNPN* (as explicitly stated in Clause 8.2.19.15 and reinforced by Clause 4.6.1, which states: *"If the UE is registering or registered for onboarding services in SNPN, the serving SNPN shall not provide a configured NSSAI to the UE"*).

This occurs during registration or configuration update procedures to:
- Enable the UE to form appropriate **Requested NSSAI** for subsequent registrations or PDU session establishment;
- Support subscription-based restrictions (e.g., NSSRG/NSAG);
- Replace any previously stored Configured NSSAI for that SNPN (per Clause 4.6.2.2a), ensuring alignment with current network policy.

It is **not sent during onboarding registration or while onboarded**, as the SNPN does not configure slices for onboarding UEs — slice configuration is deferred until post-onboarding registration.

Upon receiving a SERVICE REJECT with 5GMM cause #28 ("Restricted service area") over 3GPP access when the service type was not set to "elevated signalling", the UE shall:

1. Enter the state **5GMM-REGISTERED.NON-ALLOWED-SERVICE**;  
2. Wait for the release of the N1 NAS signalling connection;  
3. Perform the **registration procedure for mobility and periodic registration update** as specified in subclause 5.5.1.3.

This behaviour ensures the UE re-registers upon moving to an allowed area, in accordance with restricted service area handling.

The network must include the Truncated 5G-S-TMSI configuration IE **only when the UE is in NB-N1 mode** and all of the following conditions are met:
- The UE requests "control plane CIoT 5GS optimization" in the 5GS update type IE of the REGISTRATION REQUEST;
- The AMF accepts the requested CIoT 5GS optimization;
- The network is configured to provide the truncated 5G-S-TMSI configuration for control plane CIoT 5GS optimizations.

This is explicitly mandated in Clauses 8.2.7.37 and 5.5.1.2.4 (and confirmed in 5.5.1.3.4), which state inclusion is conditional on the UE being in NB-N1 mode — not WB-N1, not general N1 mode, and not during generic configuration updates unless specifically for NB-N1 + CP-CIoT.

Upon receiving a message with 5GMM cause #77 ("wireline access area not allowed") over a wireline access network, the **5G-RG must**:

1. Set the **5GS update status to `5U3 ROAMING NOT ALLOWED`** and store it per clause 5.1.3.2.2;  
2. **Delete** the 5G-GUTI, last visited registered TAI, TAI list, and ngKSI;  
3. **Reset the registration attempt counter**;  
4. **Enter the state `5GMM-DEREGISTERED`** (for wireline access);  
5. **Cease all NAS signalling over that wireline access network**, and **not reattempt registration or service requests over that wireline access** until one of the following occurs:  
   - the 5G-RG is **switched off**,  
   - the **UICC containing the USIM is removed**, or  
   - the 5G-RG **starts using another wireline access network**  
   (as mandated by Clause 5.3.23, item a).

These actions are specific to the wireline access type and do not affect registration state over other access types (e.g., 3GPP), per the independence principle in Note 5 of Clause 5.5.2.3.2 and Clause 5.3.23.

The AMF:  
1. Sets the **EAP message IE** to the EAP-request message;  
2. Sets the **ngKSI IE** to the selected ngKSI value (per clauses 5.4.1.2.2.2, 5.4.1.2.3.1, or 5.4.1.2.3A.1);  
3. Sets the **ABBA IE** to length 2 with value `0000H`;  
4. Sends the AUTHENTICATION REQUEST message to the UE;  
5. Starts **timer T3560**.  

(Per Clause 5.4.1.2.4.2 — no further actions are taken *immediately after creation* before transmission and timer start.)

The UE constructs the **NETWORK SLICE-SPECIFIC AUTHENTICATION COMPLETE** message by:

- Setting the **EAP message IE** to the provided EAP-response message;  
- Setting the **S-NSSAI IE** to the corresponding **SNPN S-NSSAI** (i.e., the SNPN-specific S-NSSAI associated with that EAP-response);  
- Sending the message — with no further 5GMM-layer involvement, as the procedure is transparent to 5GMM (Clause 5.4.7.2.2 [2]).

A UE is allowed to initiate 5GMM procedures even if timer T3346 is running or deactivated in the following cases (per clauses 5.3.9, 5.2.2.3.3, 5.2.3.2.3, and 5.2.2.3.1):

- For **emergency services** or **emergency services fallback**, regardless of access type (3GPP/non-3GPP);  
- If the UE is **configured for high priority access** in the selected PLMN;  
- Upon **reception of a paging message or NOTIFICATION message**, which *stops* T3346 and triggers immediate response (e.g., service request or registration update);  
- To perform **local de-registration and initiate initial registration for emergency services** (even while T3346 is running);  
- To initiate **UE-requested PDU session release** (per clause 6.4.3.1 — explicitly permitted despite T3346, T3396, T3584, T3585);  
- To initiate **UE-requested PDU session modification** *only* for changing 3GPP PS data off status — this is mandatory even if T3346 (or other back-off timers) is running or deactivated (clause 6.4.2.1);  
- For **NB-N1 mode exception data reporting**, if allowed and T3346 is not already running for “MO exception data” (clauses 5.2.2.3.3 & 5.2.3.2.3).  

Note: Initiation of *other* 5GMM procedures (e.g., periodic/mobility registration update, initial registration for non-emergency purposes) is **not permitted** while T3346 is running — they are deferred until expiry unless one of the above exceptions applies.

If the UE experiences a transmission failure of the **NETWORK SLICE-SPECIFIC AUTHENTICATION COMPLETE** message and the current TAI is not in the TAI list, it shall:

- **Abort** the network slice-specific authentication and authorization procedure;  
- If the UE is in **5GMM-REGISTERED** state, initiate a **registration procedure for mobility and periodic registration update** (indicating "mobility registration updating" in the 5GS registration type);  
- Otherwise, initiate an **initial registration procedure**.

*(Based on TS 24.501, Clause 5.4.7.2.4, case a)*

A UE may include the **Requested PEIPS assistance information IE** in the REGISTRATION REQUEST message **if and only if** all of the following conditions are met:

- The UE supports NR paging subgrouping;  
- The UE is **not performing initial registration for emergency services**;  
- The UE is **not registered for emergency services**; and  
- The UE **does not have an active emergency PDU session** (over 3GPP access).  

*(Ref: Clause 8.2.6.38 [4] and reinforced by Clauses 5.3.25 [1] and 8.2.7.42 [2])*
